Welcome to notebook twelve. By this point you have used generics, variance, bounds, and givens. Enough to write most of the Scala you will meet day to day. This notebook adds the smaller, sharper tools that Scala three introduces on top of that foundation. None of them are required to write working code. All of them, used well, make wrong code stop compiling.

The toolbox covers eight ideas. Type aliases. Shorter names for long types. Opaque types. Distinct types at compile time, free at runtime. Literal and singleton types. Using a specific value as a type. Union types, written A pipe B, meaning either of, without wrapping. Intersection types, written A ampersand B, meaning both of, combined. Top and bottom types. Any, AnyVal, AnyRef, Nothing, and Null. Match types. Type level pattern matching. And structural types. Typing by shape. The goal is not to use everything here in every program. The goal is to recognise each tool when you see it in a library, and reach for the right one when modelling a domain.

Let us start with type aliases. A type alias gives an existing type a second name. It introduces no new type. The alias and the original are exactly the same type to the compiler. The only purpose is readability. So writing type UserId equals Long means UserId and Long are interchangeable everywhere. You can pass a UserId where a Long is expected and the other way around. Writing type Headers equals Map of String and List of String is a longer payoff. It hides a long type behind a short name.

The trap with aliases is using them to strengthen a weak type rather than shorten a long one. Writing type UserId equals Long does not stop a caller from passing any other Long where a UserId is expected. The alias is transparent. For real strengthening, where mixing up a UserId and an OrderId becomes a compile error, you want an opaque type.

A real world problem first. Code that traffics in Long typed ids, String typed emails, and Int typed amounts can mix them up at the call site with no compiler complaint. A function transfer of amount comma user id and a function transfer of user id comma amount both type check when both are Long. This is primitive obsession, and it causes a steady stream of subtle bugs. An opaque type is the fix. It declares a new type that the compiler treats as distinct from its underlying representation, while still compiling down to the underlying type at runtime. Zero allocation. Zero indirection.

You declare an opaque type inside an enclosing object or package. Write opaque type UserId equals Long. Then on a companion object UserId, declare an apply method that takes a Long and returns a UserId, plus an extension method called value that returns the underlying Long. Do the same for OrderId. Now outside the enclosing object, UserId and OrderId are completely distinct. The expression a user id plus an order id does not compile. You have to call dot value on each to get back to the raw Long.

Three things to register about opaque types. First, inside the defining scope, here the object Ids, UserId and Long are interchangeable. The implementation can freely convert between them. Second, outside that scope, they are completely distinct. Passing a raw Long where a UserId is expected fails to compile. Third, at runtime there is no wrapper class. A UserId is, in memory, just a Long. You get type safety with no boxing cost, which matters in hot loops and large collections. This is the same idea as Haskell's newtype, or Rust's tuple struct wrapper, with the Scala specific twist that the scope of opaque to whom is the enclosing scope of the definition.

Next, literal types. A specific value as a type. Most types describe kinds of values. Int describes all integers. A literal type describes a single one. The literal forty two is also a type, written as forty two, inhabited by exactly one value. The value forty two. So you can write val answer colon forty two equals forty two. Writing val wrong colon forty two equals forty three does not compile. Forty three is not of type forty two. Combined with unions, literal types let you express tiny string enums. Like type YesNo equals the string yes pipe the string no. A YesNo value can be exactly the string yes or the string no. Anything else is a compile error.

Literal types shine in two places. First, as the building blocks of string enum like APIs. The string yes pipe the string no is a tiny union of two literal types. Second, in libraries that compute types from values. A request handler that promises to return JSON might be typed Handler of the string application slash json. You rarely declare them by hand outside of API boundaries.

Closely related. Singleton types, written name dot type. Every value has a singleton type, accessible via dot type. It describes the one element set containing just that value. So if you declare object Logger, then val l colon Logger dot type equals Logger compiles. Writing val other colon Logger dot type equals a new Object does not compile. Only Logger itself fits. You will meet singleton types most often as the type of object declarations. Logger dot type is the type of the Logger object itself. They also show up in DSLs and path dependent typing, but those are niche.

Now to the most useful headline feature of the Scala three type system. Union types. A union type A pipe B is the type of values that are either an A or a B. Unlike Either, there is no wrapping. No Left or Right constructor. You just have a value whose static type is the union. So you can write def describe taking an x of type Int pipe String, returning a string, with two cases. Case n colon Int arrow the string int followed by n. Case s colon String arrow the string string of length followed by s dot length. Describe of forty two returns int forty two. Describe of the string scala returns string of length five.

When you match on a union, the compiler narrows each branch to the specific member type. So in the case n colon Int branch, n is an Int. Full stop. No cast. No runtime check needed beyond the match itself.

Common uses for unions. First, return or error without Either. A parser can return Token pipe ParseError and let the caller handle each. Second, heterogeneous parameters. A logging function that accepts String pipe Throwable. Third, literal enums. The strings GET pipe POST pipe PUT pipe DELETE is a perfectly good HTTP method type. How do you choose between unions and Either. Reach for Either when which side it is carries meaning. Success versus failure, where you want to chain with map and flat map. Reach for a union when the alternatives are simply different shapes the same value can take.

Now the mirror image. Intersection types, written A ampersand B. The type of values that satisfy both A and B. They implement everything in A and everything in B. So a function instrumented, taking a parameter svc of type Logger ampersand Metrics, requires the caller to supply something that is both a logger and a metrics collector. Inside the function, all methods from both traits are available without any casting. This is the modern replacement for the with style trait composition in signatures. Same idea. Cleaner notation.

There is a nice algebraic intuition here. Unions and intersections form an algebra on types that mirrors or and and on booleans. A pipe A is A. A ampersand A is A. A pipe B is the same as B pipe A. The order does not matter. Likewise for ampersand. Intersections distribute over unions. This algebra is the basis for match types coming up below. Types that compute based on the shape of other types.

Let us step back and look at Scala's type hierarchy as a whole. At the top sits Any. Every value is an Any. Below Any branches into two halves. AnyVal, which covers java virtual machine primitives. Int, Boolean, Double, Char, Unit, and so on. And AnyRef, which covers reference types. Anything from java dot lang dot Object downwards. AnyRef is equivalent to java's Object. Below AnyRef sits Null. The type of the null literal. It is a subtype of every reference type but not of AnyVal. With Scala three's null safe mode enabled, this connection is severed and null becomes a discipline rather than a default. Finally, at the very bottom sits Nothing. The bottom type. A subtype of every type. It has no values at all.

Nothing looks useless at first. A type you cannot construct. Its job is to represent no value at all. Which makes it the return type of functions that never return, because they always throw or loop forever. And the element type of empty generic containers like Nil and None. So def crash, taking a message, returning Nothing, equals throw a new RuntimeException, has return type Nothing because the function never produces a value. And because Nothing is a subtype of every type, crash can stand in anywhere. You can write val n colon Int equals if true then one else crash, and the type checker is happy. Same with val s colon String equals if true then ok else crash.

The same property explains why Nil works for every list. Nil has type List of Nothing. Because List is covariant, and Nothing is a subtype of Int, List of Nothing is a subtype of List of Int. The empty list works as the empty version of every list type at once. That is only possible because Nothing sits at the bottom of the hierarchy. This neatly closes the loop with the variance discussion from notebook ten.

Now to a more niche feature. Match types. Pattern matching at the type level. Match types let you compute a type by pattern matching on another type. They are rare in application code. You mostly read them rather than write them. But understanding the shape is helpful when a library leans on them. So you can write type Element of X equals X match. Case String arrow Char. Case Array of t arrow t. Case Iterable of t arrow t. Read it like a value level match, but on the right side of an equals sign and operating on types. Element of String reduces to Char. Element of Array of Int reduces to Int. Element of List of Boolean reduces to Boolean.

The standard library provides a type equality witness called the equals colon equals operator. Writing summon of A equals colon equals B compiles only if A and B are the same type after all reductions. Match types make APIs like tuple head, init and last on tuples, and various map a tuple of types combinators possible. You will meet them in advanced library code, especially around generic derivation. In application code, leave them alone unless you have a clear reason.

A brief mention of structural types for completeness. A structural type describes what methods an object must have, without naming a specific class or trait. So a function could take a parameter whose type is, in curly braces, def close returning Unit. Any value with a close method matches, regardless of whether it implements a Closeable trait. Behind the scenes, the java virtual machine uses reflection to make the call, which is why this carries a performance cost and is not the default. Mention it here so you recognise it. Reach for nominal types, that is, traits, in almost every real situation.

Let us close with a small example that pulls together opaque types, a union, an extension, and an intersection. Inside an object called Money, declare opaque type Cents equals Long, with a companion object that provides an apply method and two extensions. A plus operator that adds two Cents values. And a toDollars method that divides by one hundred. Outside Money, you have a distinct Cents type with arithmetic and a display conversion, but at runtime it is still a Long. Next, an enum AuthError with two cases. Unauthenticated and Forbidden with a reason string. A case class Account with an id and a balance of type Cents. And a function lookup, taking a token, returning the union type Account pipe AuthError. Either the account, or one of two named errors. To consume the result, pattern match. Case acct colon Account arrow the balance string. Case AuthError dot Unauthenticated arrow please log in. Case AuthError dot Forbidden of why arrow forbidden colon why.

Notice the three features doing different jobs. Opaque Cents stops anyone from accidentally storing dollars or millicents in the same field. Inside Money, it is a plain Long. Outside, it is a distinct type with controlled operations. The union Account pipe AuthError lets the function return either a success or a structured error without wrapping in Either. The caller pattern matches and is checked for exhaustiveness because AuthError is sealed. Extension methods on Cents provide plus and toDollars without making Cents a wrapper class. At runtime, the addition is a primitive Long add. Used together, this is what modern Scala three domain modelling looks like. Precise types at compile time. Plain primitives at runtime.

Notebook thirteen leaves the type system behind and moves to runtime concerns. Futures and concurrency. Future of A is a map and flat map shaped container just like the ones from notebook nine. But its values are produced eventually. So composition rules carry over while the semantics shift. After that, notebook fourteen brings the error handling and resource management story together. And notebook fifteen lands at the Spark shaped use of everything you have built up.
